@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Octo 4: functions and function handles)

Functions package work for reuse; handles let you pass a function around as a value.

@section(Anonymous functions)

`@(x) ...` makes a one-line function that captures the variables around it.

@octo
k = 3;
f = @(x) k * x.^2;
f(2), f([1 2 3])
g = @(x, y) x + y; g(2, 5)
h = @sin; h(pi/2)
func2str(f), class(f), is_function_handle(f)
s = str2func("@(t) t + 1"); s(1)
feval(@max, [4 9 2])
@end

@section(Script functions)

A block can define named functions after its script lines. `nargin` and default values handle optional arguments; several outputs come back in brackets.

@octo
[lo, hi] = bounds2([4 8 1 6])
area = circle_area(2)
area1 = circle_area()

function [a, b] = bounds2(v)
  a = min(v);
  b = max(v);
end

function a = circle_area(r)
  if nargin < 1
    r = 1;
  end
  a = pi * r^2;
end
@end

@section(Recursion and varargin)

A function may call itself; `varargin` collects any number of arguments.

@octo
fib(10)
total(1, 2, 3, 4)

function f = fib(n)
  if n < 2
    f = n;
  else
    f = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
  end
end

function s = total(varargin)
  s = 0;
  for k = 1:numel(varargin)
    s = s + varargin{k};
  end
  printf("%d arguments\n", nargin);
end
@end

@section(Applying functions to arrays)

`arrayfun` and `cellfun` map a function over elements; `accumarray` sums values by group.

@octo
arrayfun(@(n) n^2, 1:5)
cellfun(@upper, {"a", "b"}, "UniformOutput", false)
accumarray([1; 2; 1; 3], [10; 20; 30; 40])'
@end
